  "etymologyNotes": [
    {
      "s": "makemeahanzi",
      "t": "A pregnant woman"
    },
    {
      "s": "dong_chinese",
      "t": "Pictograph of a person with an protruding midsection. The protruding midsection is variously explained as (1) representing the main part of a person's body, or (2) as representing a pregnant woman that has a body inside her body."
    },
    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "躳也。象人之身。从人𠂆聲。凡身之屬皆从身。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Pictogram (象形): from a pictograph of a pregnant woman. See also the reversed version 㐆. Unrelated to 射.\nSTEDT relates it to Proto-Sino-Tibetan *sja-n ~ *sin (“flesh; animal; body”), but recent Old Chinese reconstructions would not support this etymology.\nBecause 身 is used as a phonetic for 仁 (OC *niŋ) in excavated texts, Baxter and Sagart (2012, 2014) reconstruct the initial as nasal. This would allow for a comparison to Tibetan སྙིང (snying, “heart”), Japhug tɯsni (“heart”) (Baxter and Sagart, 2012; Zhang, Jacques and Lai, 2019), which are from Proto-Sino-Tibetan *s-niŋ (“heart; mind; brain”)."
    }
  ],
  "shuowen": "躳也。象人之身。从人𠂆聲。凡身之屬皆从身。",
  "shuowenExplanation": "body. Composed of 人 \"man\" (semantic) and 𠂆 \"to drag\" (phonetic). All characters in the 身 \"body\" category use this as radical.",
